Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||09-21-2001||06:08 PM||||Fieroryan is quite right , once you get to that degree of modification year doesn't become a factor.<P>The quality and effectivness of the mods on the other hand  do become a big factor.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||09-22-2001||02:04 AM||||The only advantage an 85 Fiero would have over an 88 is only a slightly easier time finding parts. The curse of a single model year suspension<P>Though it seems to be easier to find Model A parts than a Fiero part.||||reg||9
Z||000006||Capt Fiero||09-22-2001||02:20 AM||||The first big thing that all 88's have over the pre 88's is a rear sway bar.  When 2 people drive both cars, they all go wowo over the 88 and how it is so much more responsive.  Well most of that "normal driving" feel is due to the rear bar.    I am not saying the 88 is not superior to the 87 in regards to bump steer or anything else.   My dream was to have an 88 Fiero to build.  Well after being in Fiero's for 6.5 years now, I will take a pre 88 and mod it for 1/2 the price of buying an 88 original car, and end up with a car better suited to my driving style in the end. (plus replacment parts I can find and afford)  If it was not for the rarity and difficulty to find parts for the 88 brakes and suspension as well as upgrade parts are fare and few between.  I would still be looking for an 88.  In short, if you want a Fiero to drive and never work on then maybe an 88 is for you.  But if you want a fiero to build, mod, race, abuse (maybe a bad choice in words) but in general drive hard. The pre 85-87 V6 cars are your best starting platforms.  I apologize if I pissed a few of you off. Now to answer your original question.   I agree with the others, when you get to that level of mods, it really does not matter what year you start with the results will be pretty close to the same.<P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubimage.asp?id_=32415"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||BlackDragon||09-22-2001||02:20 AM||||total agreement on model a parts being easier to find.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>i am wanting to do something similar. i have an 84 coupe. i think im going to buy an 84 se for the power options, and convert it to a fastback, get the koni racing kit from the fieroshop, engine swap... im thinking LS1, not sure yet. 6 spd quaife. 19x10 rear 18x8 fronts with eakle f1 rubber. stage 1 from ausi. chop. nice wing. all polly. blah blah blah blah blah blah...you have heard this kind of setup im sure. <BR>but i wasnt sure if i should get an 88 or not. i figured if you hadd all of the suspension mods, it wouldnt matter though, because every mod you do to an 84, you can do to the 88, and they will both be at the same level, but the 84 will have had a bigger change.<P>just my two sence. all that realy says is "85 will be just as good as 88"<P>sorry for the long post   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>he he he, guess it wasnt as long as i thought  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"><P>------------------<BR>We are all different for a reason.....<BR>you can read a persons soul through there eyes.....<P><BR>BlackDragon<p>[This message has been edited by BlackDragon (edited 09-22-2001).]||||reg||1
